## Describe the bug

The statement splitter in `Migrator` cuts generated SQL on `;\n` and re-merges chunks that land inside a single-quoted literal, but it doesn't know about dollar-quoted blocks. A `@Trigger({ expression })` holding a multi-line PL/pgSQL function therefore ends up spread over several `addSql()` calls, and `migration:up` fails with `42601 unterminated dollar-quoted string`.

Same root cause as #8061. That was fixed by normalizing `trigger.body` (#8063), but `createTrigger()` returns `expression` verbatim, so the escape-hatch form still breaks. #8062, which fixed the splitter itself, was closed. Still reproduces on 7.1.11.

The practical effect is that the generated migration can't be replayed — running `migration:create` and then `migration:up` on a fresh database fails.

Workaround: keep the whole function body on one line.

## Reproduction

No repo, sorry. `tests/issues/GH8061.test.ts` reproduces it if the trigger is declared with `expression` (the full `create or replace function ... $$ ... $$;` + `create trigger ...` DDL, body spanning multiple lines) instead of `body`.

## Suggested fix

Track `$$` / `$tag$` in the splitter the same way single quotes already are: while the buffer holds an odd number of dollar-quote tags, keep accumulating instead of ending the statement. Essentially what #8062 did.

```diff
--- a/packages/migrations/src/Migrator.ts
+++ b/packages/migrations/src/Migrator.ts
@@ -390,6 +390,11 @@ const splitStatements = (sql: string) => {
         // odd number of single quotes means we're inside a string literal
         if (buf.split(`'`).length % 2 === 0) {
           continue;
         }

+        // odd number of dollar-quote tags means we're inside a dollar-quoted body
+        if ((buf.match(/\$(?:[A-Za-z_]\w*)?\$/g) ?? []).length % 2 === 1) {
+          continue;
+        }
+
         // A chunk starting with \n indicates there was a blank line (grouping separator)
         if (buf.startsWith('\n')) {
           result.push('');
         }
```

That's what I'm running as a patch locally and it fixes both the generated migration and `schema:update`.

