## Describe the bug

`Routine.is()` still uses a bare `instanceof` check, so it breaks under the **CJS/ESM dual-package hazard** — the same problem #7289 fixed for `EntitySchema.is()`, but `Routine.is()` was not given the equivalent duck-typing fallback.

```ts
// packages/core/src/metadata/Routine.ts  (master)
static is(item: unknown): item is Routine {
  return item instanceof Routine;
}
```

vs the hardened version:

```ts
// packages/core/src/metadata/EntitySchema.ts  (after #7289)
static is(item: unknown): item is EntitySchema {
  if (item instanceof EntitySchema) {
    return true;
  }
  return item != null && typeof item === 'object'
      && item.constructor?.name === 'EntitySchema' && 'meta' in item;
}
```

When a TypeScript config loader (e.g. `tsx`) loads the ORM config in a different module realm than the CLI, the config's `Routine` instances are not `instanceof` the CLI's `Routine` class. `Configuration.normaliseRoutines()` then throws:

```
Error: 'routines' entry is not a stored routine declaration. Use a Routine class instance.
    at Configuration.normaliseRoutines (.../@mikro-orm/core/utils/Configuration.js)
```

So configs that declare `routines: [...]` still fail under exactly the loaders #7289 was meant to unblock. `EntitySchema`-only configs work; the moment a stored routine is registered, the CLI breaks.

## Reproduction

1. MikroORM v7 (`@mikro-orm/core@7.1.9`), a `"type": "commonjs"` project.
2. A config declaring at least one stored routine:
   ```ts
   export default defineConfig({
     // ...
     routines: [myRoutine], // a `new Routine(...)` instance
   });
   ```
3. Run any CLI command through `tsx`, e.g. `MIKRO_ORM_CLI_TS_LOADER=tsx mikro-orm migration:list` (or run the CLI under `tsx`).

→ throws `'routines' entry is not a stored routine declaration. Use a Routine class instance.`

The same config + command with `MIKRO_ORM_CLI_TS_LOADER=jiti` works, because jiti loads `@mikro-orm/core` as a single instance — confirming it's the dual-package hazard, not an invalid routine declaration.

## Expected behavior

`Routine.is()` should tolerate cross-realm instances the way `EntitySchema.is()` does (post-#7289), so `routines`-using configs load under `tsx`/`swc` like the rest of the config after #7289.

## Suggested fix

Mirror the `EntitySchema.is()` fallback — `Routine` instances carry a `type` discriminator:

```ts
static is(item: unknown): item is Routine {
  if (item instanceof Routine) {
    return true;
  }
  return item != null && typeof item === 'object'
      && item.constructor?.name === 'Routine' && 'type' in item;
}
```

