### Describe the bug

`renderPartialIndexWhere` (used by `@Unique`/`@Index` with a `where` predicate to generate a partial index) throws a false positive — "`where` references another table or subquery which cannot be inlined into a `CREATE INDEX ... WHERE` clause" — when the entity has a non-lazy `@Formula` property, even though the actual `where` predicate never references another table.

Root cause: `renderPartialIndexWhere` builds a synthetic query with `createQueryBuilder(entityName, ..., alias)` + `qb.where(where)`, then calls `qb.getFormattedQuery()`. Because no explicit `.select()` is set, the query builder falls back to `select "__p".*`, which pulls in any non-lazy `@Formula` properties — including their own embedded subqueries. The predicate is then extracted from the SQL string via `/\bwhere\s+([\s\S]+)$/i` — matching the **first** `where` keyword in the whole string. If a `@Formula` subquery contains its own `where` (e.g. `(select count(*) from book b where b.author_id = alias.id)`), that inner `where` is matched instead of the real top-level predicate, so everything from there to the end of the string (including the formula's legitimate cross-table references, plus the real predicate) gets checked for cross-table references — and incorrectly rejected.

A one-line fix that resolves it locally: force the synthetic query builder to select a constant instead of the default `*`, e.g. `qb.select(raw('1'))`, right after creating the `qb` and before `qb.where(where)`. That keeps `@Formula` properties out of the generated SQL entirely, since we only care about the `FROM ... WHERE` clause for this purpose.

https://github.com/mikro-orm/mikro-orm/blob/v7.1.5/packages/sql/src/AbstractSqlDriver.ts#L2964-L3027

