I'm currently migrating a project from V6 to V7 and I try to use defineEntity instead of the classic class + decorator method.

I ran into a few small issues, and I'd like to understand how specific migration cases should be handled. For some cases I think some documentation update might be helpful.

Some background: I'm purely constructing my entities through the classes extended from `FooSchema.class`, never using `em.create()`.

a) The documentation on default values is not perfectly clear, but as far as I understood it, this is the correct approach to handle this, in order for the properties to be immedietly available before persisting:

```ts
const MySchema = defineEntity({
    name: "My",
    properties: {
        id: p.uuid().primary(),
    },
});

export class My extends MySchema.class {
    id = v7();
}
MySchema.setClass(My);
```

b) Originally an entity was not extending any other class, so TypeScript would tell you when your constructor is not setting all non-optional values, so you had compile time safety. Since we now have to call super() first, I assume there is no compile time safety anymore for that, unless you'd use em.create()? Though I highly prefer custom constructors which can do some more logic.

In regards to `em.create()`, in the example above, when I do `em.create()`, it does identify that the "id" field is not required. Though if I do any other type which is set via field property in my extending class, `em.create()` still requires that field to be passed. I assume there is simply some short-circuit logic for primary keys which excludes them?

c) In regards to nullable values, in my old entity classes I always typed them as `SomeType | null`, and my constructor made sure that they can only be one of the two. Now when doing `p.something().nullable()`, they become both `null` AND `undefined`. Considering that my constructor ensures that they cannot be undefined, this makes a lot of other code places really akward, as now I'd have to always convert undefined to null, even though that can never actually happen.  In the documentation I found the following for optional properties:

```ts
  properties: {
    title: p.string().$type<RequiredNullable<string>>(),
}
```

Beside that being really cumbersome to define for every nullable field, this one does not, as far as I can see, tell the schema manager that the field should allow NULL values, right? I also tried `p.string().nullable().$type<…>()`, but because the nullable option persists, the type override does not influence the `undefined` behavior.

Maybe there could be a `strictNullable()` option to specifically force nullability without `undefined`? Or potentially a boolean option passed to `nullable(true)`.
